{
  "image_name": "0f51ab1d8d35d899b44b3399862941c5.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how different immune-cell metabolic states influence heart cells during injury or stress. It centers on macrophage subtypes and related immune cells, showing that their fuel use shapes the signals they release and the effects on cardiomyocytes and fibroblasts.\n\nOn the left, anti-inflammatory macrophages rely on fatty-acid β-oxidation and produce anti-inflammatory cytokines such as IL-13. These signals act on cardiomyocytes to support glucose metabolism and also appear to influence fibroblasts. In contrast, pro-inflammatory macrophages use glucose, convert pyruvate to lactate, and release inflammatory cytokines like TNF and IL-1β. During myocardial ischaemia, this inflammatory program is linked to lactate accumulation, which enters cardiomyocytes through MCT1 and is associated with dedifferentiation.\n\nIn the middle, lactate and succinate also activate resident macrophages and Treg cells through labeled receptors, suggesting metabolite sensing as a key mechanism. On the right, lipid-rich states appear to activate specific resident macrophage populations: increased fatty acids in cardiometabolic HFpEF activate TIMD4-positive resident macrophages, which are connected to cardiomyocyte hypertrophy and apoptosis, while in sepsis dysfunctional mitochondria released as exophers seem to stimulate TREM2-high resident macrophages.\n\nOverall, the image links immune-cell metabolism to inflammation, tissue remodeling, and cardiac dysfunction.",
